  "content": {
    "id": "p14-cc-6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "The derivative of $x^3+x^2$ with respect to $x$ is $3x^2+2x$.\n\nExpress this using four different notations.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^3+x^2)=3x^2+2x$. If $y=x^3+x^2$, then $y'=3x^2+2x$ and $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=3x^2+2x$. If $f(x)=x^3+x^2$, then $f'(x)=3x^2+2x$.",
      "worked": "The four equivalent forms are\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(x^3+x^2)&=3x^2+2x,\\\\y'&=3x^2+2x,\\\\\\frac{dy}{dx}&=3x^2+2x,\\\\f'(x)&=3x^2+2x.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor the middle two forms, $y=x^3+x^2$; for the last form, $f(x)=x^3+x^2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 54
  },
